<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Weser</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as River Weser</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Weser is a river of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, formed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hann-munden">Hann. Münden</a> by the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werra">Werra</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a> and flowing about 450 km north through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a> past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremerhaven">Bremerhaven</a>. In Roman times it ran through the lands of the Cherusci and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>, and later it carried the trade of the Hanseatic port of Bremen.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/weser.jpg" alt="Weser" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Arminia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weser2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
